  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Anolignan B interacts with Phosphodiesterase 6C through a series of molecular pathways involving the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex. This complex is a target of multiple anolignan derivatives, including Anolignan B, which are structurally related to a naphthalene derivative and another trimethylpentadecanone. These compounds are all capable of binding to the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex, which in turn targets Phosphodiesterase 6C. The consistent scores across the three paths indicate a strong, reliable relationship between Anolignan B and Phosphodiesterase 6C, likely mediated through the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex. The evidence supports a mechanistic link where the anolignans act as modulators of the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex, which then influences the activity of Phosphodiesterase 6C. The pathways are consistent, suggesting a general mechanism of action, though the exact nature of the interaction between Anolignan B and the complex remains to be fully elucidated.",
